-- title:
--   One-place idele with local elementary divisors (1,ℓ) is a prime Hecke element
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ with $a\neq 0$ and $b\neq 0$, and let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of the quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ satisfying `IsOrder`: it contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, and is finitely generated. Let $v$ be a height-one prime of the ring of integers of $\mathbb{Q}$ and $\ell$ a prime natural number with the image of $\ell$ lying in $v$, so that $v$ is the place above $\ell$. Let $\varphi$ be a ring isomorphism $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v\cong M_2(\mathbb{Q}_v)$ carrying $1\otimes r$ to the scalar matrix $r\cdot 1$, and assume that $\varphi$ identifies the local box of $\Lambda$ at $v$ — the additive subgroup generated by the elements $z\otimes c$ with $z\in\Lambda$ and $c$ in the valuation ring of $\mathbb{Q}_v$ — with the set of elements all of whose matrix entries under $\varphi$ are integral. Let $k_1,k_2$ be invertible $2\times 2$ matrices over $\mathbb{Q}_v$ such that all entries of $k_1,k_1^{-1},k_2,k_2^{-1}$ are integral. Let $s$ be a unit of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\widehat{\mathbb{Q}}$, where $\widehat{\mathbb{Q}}$ is the finite adele ring, whose image under the base-change evaluation map at $v$ equals $\varphi^{-1}(k_1\,\mathrm{diag}(1,\ell)\,k_2)$ and whose image under the evaluation map at every $w\neq v$ equals $1$. Then $s$ lies in `primeHeckeSet` $\Lambda$ $\ell$: writing $\widehat{\Lambda}$ for the finite adele box of $\Lambda$, the additive subgroup generated by the $z\otimes\alpha$ with $z\in\Lambda$ and $\alpha$ an integral finite adele, one has $s\in\widehat{\Lambda}$, $\ell\cdot s^{-1}\in\widehat{\Lambda}$, $s^{-1}\notin\widehat{\Lambda}$ and $\ell^{-1}\cdot s\notin\widehat{\Lambda}$.
--
--   This is the local recognition step for the Hecke correspondence at $\ell$: an idele concentrated at the place above $\ell$, with local elementary divisors $(1,\ell)$ relative to a maximal-looking local lattice, satisfies the four integrality and non-integrality conditions defining a prime Hecke element of $\Lambda$ at $\ell$. It is used in the factorisation of a normalised connecting idele into prime Hecke steps.
